That's not really what I'm saying. Of course in general the game needs to 
be different, and obviously seeding doesn't work on a repeat play of a 
dungeon. All I'm really suggesting is that it would be interesting to 
allow people to tackle the same dungeon and compare results, in the same 
way that bones files can be fun aside from the loot you gain. That doesn't 
mean you have to play the same dungeon each time. The first time you 
play a seeded game, it would be just as new as an unseeded game.

Ever play a version of Solitaire where after a game you could get the card 
shuffling algorithm's seed and challenge somebody else to beat your score? 
Think along those lines.

